# Development workflow

Principle: **Challenge early, decide explicitly, implement deliberately.**

Full protocol (phases, Cross Design Review severities, Done criteria) lives in Notion — Forge **Workflow de développement** (SoT) and the project's Workflow application page when present. Do not duplicate that narrative here.

## Cursor modes (part of the protocol)

- **Ask** — analysis / investigation / audit **without modifying** the project.
- **Plan** — inspect the repository and build an implementation plan **without implementing**.
- **Agent** — execute a **validated** plan: implementation, tests, verification, and documentation sync.

Respect the mode the user selected. Do not implement in Ask or Plan. Do not invent a plan substitute when Agent was asked to execute an already-validated plan.

## Roles

- The human **decision maker** has final say.
- Cursor is a challenger and executor — never a silent business decision maker.

## Notion Decisions (strict)

- Do **not** decide that a business decision must be taken, and do **not** choose its content.
- If you discover a situation that needs business arbitration → **signal** it (e.g. "this needs a new business arbitration") and stop on that point.
- **After** the decision maker validates a decision → you **may** create or update the Notion Décision page to reflect the **already** arbitrated content.
- Never turn a technical hypothesis into a business decision "for documentation."

## Reference business cases

For an **important business phase**, when pertinent, expect **3–8** reference business cases defined at design time and persisted in the Notion **Phase Contract** / Spec.

- **Plan** — verify the plan can represent those cases.
- **Agent** — implement accordingly; reconstruct cases without workarounds; add automated tests only when appropriate; **flag** any case that needs bypassing invariants, internal state, or artificial abstractions.
- **Review / Reconciliation** — check coverage, observed behaviour, workarounds, and model↔code divergence.
- **Not required** for pure tech, no-behaviour refactor, trivial fix, tooling, or docs-only work.
- If unsure whether the phase needs cases → **signal**; do not invent filler cases.
- A reference case is a design/validation artefact — **not** automatically a unit test; it may become automated tests when useful.
- Do not confuse with Notion **Tasks**, roadmap phases, or product use-case catalogs.

## Scope and documentation

- Out-of-scope discoveries → Notion **Task** (flagged); do not implement them silently.
- In **Agent**, sync Notion / docs only in ways consistent with **already** validated decisions.
- Prefer linking to canonical Notion / Git sources over inventing parallel docs.
